      "text": "The bug is in the `compose` call. `csub = sub.control(1)` is a 17-qubit circuit (1 control plus the 16 subcircuit qubits), but the code maps it onto only one qubit, `[i]`. Composing needs one target qubit per circuit qubit, so the qubit list must cover all 17. I map the control to `i` and the remaining qubits to the other circuit wires in order.\n\n```python\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.circuit.library import C3XGate, C4XGate\n\nsub = QuantumCircuit(16)\nsub.cx(12, 4)\nsub.ccx(12, 4, 5)\nsub.append(C3XGate(), [4, 12, 5, 6])\nsub.append(C4XGate(), [4, 12, 5, 6, 7])\nsub.cx(13, 5)\nsub.ccx(13, 5, 6)\nsub.append(C3XGate(), [13, 5, 6, 7])\nsub.cx(14, 6)\nsub.ccx(14, 6, 7)\nsub.cx(15, 7)\n\ncsub = sub.control(1)\n\ni = 0\ncirc = QuantumCircuit(17)\n# FIX: compose got only [i] (1 qubit) for the 17-qubit controlled circuit -> pass control qubit i followed by all other 16 circuit qubits, because compose needs one target qubit per qubit of csub\ncirc.compose(csub, [i] + [q for q in range(17) if q != i], inplace=True)\n```",
